A||||7||William Federle||4 cyl water pump replacement||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||03-28-2000||10:55 PM||||I'm about to replace the water pump on my 1984 Fiero. Has anyone done this on a 4 cyl Fiero? If so did you successfully remove the pulley without the special tool? It looks as though it could be pulled on to the new pump shaft with the bolt that screws into the shaft, but taking it off of the old pump shaft might be a problem.<P>Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||03-29-2000||12:01 AM||||I would recommend using the special tool as the pulley can be a very snug fit on the shaft.  You can probably get one as a "loaner" from an auto parts store.  I know that Auto Zone will let you borrow a tool with a deposit that is refunded when the tool is returned.  My 84 did not have a threaded hole in the shaft for a bolt, so I used a press to put the pulley back on.  After two water pump changes the pulley was no longer a snug fit on the shaft so I had to get a pump with a bolt on pulley already installed.  Beware that some aftermarket pumps come with a 3 groove pulley installed. The extra groove will interfere with the wire harness that runs between the pulley and the block.  I used a cutoff wheel to remove the extra groove, but a hacksaw will also work.<BR>Have Fun!!||||reg||10
Z||000002||William Federle||04-01-2000||07:41 AM||||Rodrv6 - I did what you did - I took the water pump back and got one with the pulley already on it. It only has a 2 yr warrantee<BR>but it looks like a lot less hassle than puting my old pulley on a new pump.<P>The only thing is, how did you get your pump out? I am able to get an end-wrench on 3 or 4 bolts, but there seems to be at least that many more underneath. Did you go through the wheel-well? I have an AC compressor (that doesn't work). If you have one did you have to remove it to get to the water pump? Thanks for your trouble and answers.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||04-01-2000||05:18 PM||||The first time I changed the pump I removed the aluminum casting that the pump bolts to from the block and then removed the pump from that casting.  There are four bolts that hold it to the block and one bolt that attatches to the a/c compressor.  You will also have to remove the lower water hose and the heater hose to get it out.  It's not one of the more fun things I've done on the car!  You might be able to reach the lower pump bolts from underneath, but it can be pretty tight in there.  The only other times I've changed the pump I've had the engine out for one reason or another which makes it pretty easy.  In fact, anytime I've had the engine out I've changed the pump whether it needed it or not just so I could avoid doing it while in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||04-01-2000||08:37 PM||||Thanks. This is really the second second coolant thing I have to do. I just finished putting in a new radiator and front hoses. I was going to try to replace the pump at the same time, but I think I will wait until I have three days or so off of work. It will be easier now that I know what I have to do.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||04-08-2000||06:49 PM||||I just finished replacing the water pump. I took the engine compartment cover off. I took the pump and housing off together - that required removing the spigot that the R.H. coolant hose and heater hose are attached to. Taking this piece off allowed access to the bottom driver-side housing bolt. The other three housing bolts were no problem. I also had to take off the heat shield that covers the AC hoses. I didn't use any sealant on the new gasket between the housing and the engine since the old gasket didn't have any on it. I used the copper-colored RTV on the gasket between the water pump and the housing and a bead of RTV between the spigot and the housing when those pieces went back together. I thought for sure one of those joints would leak but so far - no leaks. This whole job would have been a lot easier if the car didn't have an AC compressor.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||04-09-2000||09:06 AM||||Here is the way "NOT" to replace the water pump on 84 2m4.  First drive a Fiero that was owned by a senior citizen and sitting idle for a year down the interstate at 75 mph.  When engine starts to knock, have it hauled home.  Remove engine (or should I say remove car).  This will give you easy access to all bolts on water pump.  Oh by the way, the pump I got from Auto Zone with a pully already installed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-10-2000||08:55 PM||||There's one thing for sure...... there ain't no easy way to do this. I said hell with it and farmed that one out. Could have done it I supose if I wasn't stuck in work clothes.||||reg||1
